Title: "Overview"
Id: "5139f041-388c-4eb4-a037-61d42dac7716"

Text: "`Requires:` records capabilities or top-level definitions a construct needs.
`Enables:` records additional capabilities, build-backed forms, and type views
that the construct supplies.

```mlg-fragment
RequiresItemUnion ::=
    | CapabilityGroup
    | DefinitionGroup

EnablesItemUnion ::=
    | CapabilityGroup
    | FromCapabilityGroup
    | FromAsGroup
    | ViewGroup
```

Ordinary capabilities from both sections participate in semantic reduction.
The split communicates whether the notation is a prerequisite or an additional
facility.

```mlg-fragment
Requires:
. capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> x_ member_of X
. definition: \natural.0 is \natural

Enables:
. capability: x_ - y_ :=> x_ \.set.minus./ y_
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ \"in\" X :<->: x_ member_of Y
. from: P ::= {(p_, q_) : ...}
  as: f(p_) := q_
. view:
  as: S := X is \set
```

An expression capability uses `:=>`. A specification capability uses `:->` for
one-way inference or `:<->:` for equivalence; multiple conclusions are
semicolon-separated. `\\abstract` is a valid abstract specification target.

A `Requires:` `definition:` item has the shape `\command is <type-or-spec>`.
It succeeds only when the command is a top-level `Defines:` item whose output
facts establish the requested relation."
Id: "db689e71-bbe1-43bf-bda4-6614f9cbe81f"
